// Title: Potential Persistence Via Custom Protocol Handler
// Sigma ID: fdbf0b9d-0182-4c43-893b-a1eaab92d085
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Nasreddine Bencherchali (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2022-05-30 | Modified: 2023-05-12
// ATT&CK: persistence, defense-impairment, T1112
// Description: Detects potential persistence activity via the registering of a new custom protocole handlers.
//   While legitimate applications register protocole handlers often times during installation. And
//   attacker can abuse this by setting a custom handler to be used as a persistence mechanism.
// False positives:
//   - Many legitimate applications can register a new custom protocol handler. Additional filters needs to applied according to your environment.
// References:
//   - https://ladydebug.com/blog/2019/06/21/custom-protocol-handler-cph/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/windows/registry/registry_set/registry_set_persistence_custom_protocol_handler.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, microsoft_xdr schema)

DeviceRegistryEvents
| where (RegistryKey =~ "HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\CLASSES*" and RegistryValueData startswith "URL:") and (not((RegistryValueData startswith "URL:ms-" or (InitiatingProcessFolderPath startswith "C:\\Program Files (x86)" or InitiatingProcessFolderPath startswith "C:\\Program Files\\" or InitiatingProcessFolderPath startswith "C:\\Windows\\System32\\" or InitiatingProcessFolderPath startswith "C:\\Windows\\SysWOW64\\"))))
